I'll use a while loop to iterate through the string, and I'll use the bitwise AND operation (`&`) to check if a bit is set to `1`. If it is, I'll swap the case of the character using Python's built-in `swapcase()` method. I'll also use the modulo operator (`%`) to cycle through the bits of `n` when we reach the end of its binary representation.

Finally, I'll return the modified string.

